Race condition. Multiple requests read same value before any write commit.

## Fix: Atomic Update

```sql
-- Bad: read then write (two trips, race window)
SELECT counter FROM table WHERE id = $1;
UPDATE table SET counter = $2 WHERE id = $1;

-- Good: atomic, single statement
UPDATE table SET counter = counter + 1 WHERE id = $1 RETURNING counter;
```

One round-trip. DB serialize writes. No race.

## Node.js Code

```js
// Bad
const { rows } = await db.query('SELECT counter FROM counters WHERE id = $1', [id]);
const newVal = rows[0].counter + 1;
await db.query('UPDATE counters SET counter = $1 WHERE id = $2', [newVal, id]);
return newVal; // concurrent requests get same rows[0].counter

// Good
const { rows } = await db.query(
  'UPDATE counters SET counter = counter + 1 WHERE id = $1 RETURNING counter',
  [id]
);
return rows[0].counter; // DB guarantees unique incremented value
```

## If Need Multi-Step Logic

Use transaction + row lock:

```js
const client = await db.connect();
try {
  await client.query('BEGIN');
  const { rows } = await client.query(
    'SELECT counter FROM counters WHERE id = $1 FOR UPDATE', // locks row
    [id]
  );
  const newVal = rows[0].counter + 1;
  await client.query(
    'UPDATE counters SET counter = $1 WHERE id = $2',
    [newVal, id]
  );
  await client.query('COMMIT');
  return newVal;
} catch (e) {
  await client.query('ROLLBACK');
  throw e;
} finally {
  client.release();
}
```

`FOR UPDATE` block other transactions until commit. Serialize access.

## Rule

| Need | Use |
|------|-----|
| Simple increment | `UPDATE ... SET x = x + 1 RETURNING x` |
| Complex logic on locked row | Transaction + `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` |
| High contention + retry OK | Optimistic lock (version column + retry on mismatch) |

Atomic update enough for most cases. Use it.
